{"id": "43:4b", "severity": "minor", "issues": [{"latin": "illuminátio vultus tui", "portuguese": "a luz do vosso rosto", "problem": "illuminatio is the act/effect of lighting up (illumination), not simply 'light' (lux/lumen); the noun is flattened.", "fix": "e a iluminação do vosso rosto"}]},
{"id": "43:10", "severity": "minor", "issues": [{"latin": "in virtútibus nostris", "portuguese": "com os nossos poderes", "problem": "virtutes here are the people's forces/hosts (armed strength); 'poderes' reads as abstract capacities. 'forças' keeps the Latin plural and its military sense without resolving it to 'exércitos'.", "fix": "e não saireis, Deus, com as nossas forças."}]},
{"id": "43:11", "severity": "minor", "issues": [{"latin": "retrórsum post inimícos nostros", "portuguese": "para trás diante dos nossos inimigos", "problem": "post means 'behind/after', not 'before/in the face of'; 'diante' follows the Hebrew/modern sense against the Latin preposition.", "fix": "Fizestes-nos voltar para trás, atrás dos nossos inimigos:"}]},
{"id": "43:17", "severity": "minor", "issues": [{"latin": "A voce exprobrántis, et obloquéntis", "portuguese": "à voz do que me afronta e me insulta", "problem": "Two participles joined by et; the translation fuses them into a single person, while the parallel second half ('do inimigo e do que me persegue') keeps two. It also supplies 'me' twice, which the Latin leaves unexpressed. Treat both halves alike.", "fix": "à voz do que afronta e do que insulta: * diante do inimigo e do que persegue."}]},
{"id": "43:22", "severity": "minor", "issues": [{"latin": "Nonne Deus requíret ista?", "portuguese": "Não pedirá Deus contas disto?", "problem": "requiret = 'will search out / seek out'; 'pedir contas' adds a judicial idiom (demand an account) that narrows the sense.", "fix": "Não investigará Deus estas coisas?"}]},
{"id": "43:23", "severity": "minor", "issues": [{"latin": "ne repéllas in finem", "portuguese": "não nos repilais para sempre", "problem": "The Latin has no object; 'nos' is supplied. Defensible from context (v.10 repulisti nos) but it adds a word the Latin leaves open.", "fix": "e não repilais para sempre."}]}
], "overall": "A faithful, close rendering: the Septuagintal features (ventilabimus cornu, declinasti semitas nostras with its positive 'et', multitudo in commutationibus, venter conglutinatus) are all kept, tenses and persons are right, and the pointing matches the Latin (one mediant per verse, no flex or second mediant in either). The faults are minor: one preposition (post) bent toward the Hebrew, a few slight narrowings (illuminatio, virtutes, requiret) and an inconsistent handling of the paired participles in v.17."}
